sudo -i
set -e
hostnamectl set-hostname "devenv"
yum install java-21-amazon-corretto -y
TOMCAT_VERSION=9.0.120
wget "https://dlcdn.apache.org/tomcat/tomcat-9/v${TOMCAT_VERSION}/bin/apache-tomcat-${TOMCAT_VERSION}.tar.gz"
tar -zxvf "apache-tomcat-${TOMCAT_VERSION}.tar.gz"
cd "apache-tomcat-${TOMCAT_VERSION}"
cat > conf/tomcat-users.xml <<'EOF'
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<tomcat-users xmlns="http://tomcat.apache.org/xml"
              xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
              xsi:schemaLocation="http://tomcat.apache.org/xml tomcat-users.xsd"
              version="1.0">
    <role rolename="manager-gui"/>
    <role rolename="manager-script"/>
    <user username="tomcat" password="admin@123" roles="manager-gui,manager-script"/>
</tomcat-users>
EOF
sed -i '/RemoteCIDRValve/,+1d' webapps/manager/META-INF/context.xml
sh bin/startup.sh

yum install git -y
===============================================================================================================================
Run these four commands one at a time:

ls -ld /root/apache-tomcat-9.0.120
cat /root/apache-tomcat-9.0.120/conf/tomcat-users.xml
ss -lntp | grep 8080
curl -I http://localhost:8080

If everything is correct:

First command → Tomcat directory exists
Second → shows manager-gui, manager-script, and tomcat
Third → shows :8080 listening
Fourth → should return an HTTP response from Tomcat

Then open:

http://YOUR-EC2-PUBLIC-IP:8080

and:

http://YOUR-EC2-PUBLIC-IP:8080/manager/html
=================================================================================================================================
On the EC2 where you are trying to log in, run these exact commands:

cd /root/apache-tomcat-9.0.120

Then:

grep -A5 -B2 '<user username' conf/tomcat-users.xml

You should see:

<role rolename="manager-gui"/>
<role rolename="manager-script"/>
<user username="tomcat" password="admin@123" roles="manager-gui,manager-script"/>

Then check the Manager restriction:

grep -n "RemoteCIDRValve\|RemoteAddrValve" webapps/manager/META-INF/context.xml

Then check whether this exact Tomcat is running:

ps -ef | grep '[o]rg.apache.catalina.startup.Bootstrap'
Most important test

From the same EC2, run:

curl -u 'tomcat:admin@123' http://localhost:8080/manager/html/

If authentication is correct, you should get HTML output rather than an HTTP 401.

Login:

Username: tomcat
Password: admin@123
